external integrations
Production configuration changes shall be controlled and auditable.

8. CONFIGURATION HIERARCHY
Configuration shall resolve through an explicit precedence hierarchy.
The initial hierarchy shall be:
1. Built-in application defaults
2. Environment configuration
3. Deployment configuration
4. Service configuration
5. Runtime-injected configuration
6. Secret provider values
Higher-priority configuration must override lower-priority defaults only through defined mechanisms.

9. CONFIGURATION SCHEMA
Configuration shall be represented through typed schemas.
Conceptually:
class Settings:
    environment: str
    database: DatabaseSettings
    storage: StorageSettings
    queue: QueueSettings
    api: APISettings
    workers: WorkerSettings
    ai: AISettings
    security: SecuritySettings
    observability: ObservabilitySettings
Configuration validation occurs before the service becomes ready.

10. FAIL-FAST CONFIGURATION VALIDATION
A service with invalid mandatory configuration shall not enter a ready state.
Example:
APPLICATION START
        ↓
LOAD CONFIGURATION
        ↓
VALIDATE CONFIGURATION
        ↓
VALID
   ↓
START SERVICE

INVALID
   ↓
FAIL STARTUP
This prevents partially configured services from entering the runtime.

