13. SECRET MANAGEMENT PRINCIPLE
Secrets shall be managed through a dedicated secret-management mechanism.
The application should retrieve secrets at deployment or startup rather than storing them in source control.
The architecture shall permit integration with:
Docker Secrets
Kubernetes Secrets
cloud secret managers
Vault-compatible systems
environment-injected secret providers
The specific production provider remains deployment-dependent.

14. SECRET STORAGE RULE
The following are prohibited:
hard-coded passwords
hard-coded API keys
hard-coded private keys
credentials committed to Git
credentials embedded in Docker images
credentials embedded in frontend bundles
credentials stored in public configuration files
Secret material must remain outside distributable application artifacts.

15. SECRET ACCESS BOUNDARY
Only services requiring a secret shall receive access to that secret.
Conceptually:
SECRET STORE
     ↓
SERVICE IDENTITY
     ↓
AUTHORIZED SECRET
     ↓
SERVICE
A frontend application shall never receive backend-only secrets.

16. SECRET EXPOSURE CONTROL
Secrets must not appear in:
application logs
error responses
API responses
telemetry
metrics
traces
exception messages
database records
generated documents
Diagnostic systems shall redact sensitive configuration automatically.

17. SECRET ROTATION
The architecture shall support secret rotation without requiring redesign of the application.
Rotatable credentials include:
database credentials
AI provider credentials
API credentials
signing keys
webhook secrets
storage credentials
integration credentials
Rotation procedures shall be documented for each production secret class.

18. KEY MANAGEMENT
Cryptographic keys shall be treated separately from ordinary configuration.
Key material may include:
JWT signing keys
encryption keys
artifact signing keys
webhook verification keys
service identity keys
Private keys shall never be exposed to client applications.

